    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/qara-yuluk-osman"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qara-yuluk-osman.jpg" alt="Qara Yuluk Osman" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/qara-yuluk-osman">Qara Yuluk Osman</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aq_Qoyunlu._Qara_Y%C3%BCl%C3%BCk_%27Uthman._AH_805-839_1403-1435_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1403-1435 CE, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aq Qoyunlu, or ‘White Sheep’ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a>, were a confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turkmen</a> tribes led by the Bayandur clan, whose flocks pastured around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a> and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in the fourteenth century. Their early chiefs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tur-ali-beg">Tur Ali Beg</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutlu-beg">Qutlu Beg</a>, first raided and then allied with the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, and Qutlu Beg married a princess of the Komnenos dynasty, beginning a long tradition of marriages between the Bayandur house and the Greek rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. The conventional starting date of 1378 marks the emergence of the confederation as a political force under Qutlu Beg’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yuluk-osman">Qara Yuluk Osman</a>, the real founder of Aq Qoyunlu power. Osman fought the Anatolian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kadi-burhan-al-din">Kadi Burhan al-Din</a>, whom he defeated and killed in 1398, and the rival Qara Qoyunlu, the ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-koyunlu">Black Sheep</a>’ Turkmens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> invaded the region Osman sided with the conqueror, and around 1402 he was rewarded with Diyarbakır, which became the core of the confederation. For three decades he extended his authority over neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> and fought the Qara Qoyunlu, until he was killed in battle against them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> in 1435. His death opened a long struggle among his sons and grandsons, which ended only when his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> made himself master of Diyarbakır in 1453.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bursa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bursa.jpg" alt="Bursa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bursa_image.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Metuboy, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aq Qoyunlu society joined two worlds. At its centre stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes, among them the Bayandur, Mawsillu and Purnak, who lived as mounted pastoralists, moving their herds between winter and summer pastures and supplying the confederation’s fighting men. Around and beneath them lived settled farmers and townspeople: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac-christians">Syriac Christians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and, after the conquests of the 1460s, the Persian-speaking population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. Agriculture in the river valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> produced most of the state’s revenue, collected as land tax and as the <em>tamgha</em>, a tax on trade and urban crafts inherited from the Mongol period. The confederation also controlled important stretches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> that carried Iranian raw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> westwards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, its capital after 1469, was one of the great commercial cities of western Asia. The ruling house was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim, but it showed favour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> married his sister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd">Shaykh Junayd</a> and his daughter to Junayd’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Haydar</a>, leaders of the Safavid order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>. Christian communities kept their churches, and a Christian queen, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-megale-komnene">Theodora Komnene</a>, known as Despina Khatun, kept her faith at Uzun Hasan’s court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-otlukbeli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-otlukbeli.jpg" alt="Battle of Otlukbeli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-otlukbeli">Battle of Otlukbeli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otlukbeli_savasi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Aq Qoyunlu army was a tribal force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, raised by the chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes and led by the ruler and his kinsmen. Its strength lay in mobility, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> used to devastating effect. In November 1467 he surprised and killed the Qara Qoyunlu ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahan-shah-qara-qoyunlu">Jahan Shah</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and in 1469 he trapped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid</a> sultan Abu Sa’id, who had invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, by cutting his supplies during the winter in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Qarabagh</a> plain; Abu Sa’id was captured and put to death. These victories made Uzun Hasan ruler of Azerbaijan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. His ambitions then turned west, where the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> had annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1461 despite Aq Qoyunlu protests. Uzun Hasan allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which sent envoys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterino-zeno">Caterino Zeno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giosafat-barbaro">Giosafat Barbaro</a> and promised firearms and artillery. In 1473 Mehmed II marched east; after an initial Aq Qoyunlu success on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, the Ottoman army, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">field artillery</a>, defeated Uzun Hasan decisively at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-otlukbeli">Battle of Otlukbeli</a> on 11 August 1473. His son Ya’qub later defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Urfa</a> in 1480, and in 1488 his forces, together with those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a>, killed the militant Safavid leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Shaykh Haydar</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oghuz-khan.jpg" alt="Oghuz Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-khan">Oghuz Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oghuz_Khan_enthroned,_folio_9r_(Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale,_Suppl%C3%A9ment_persan_1113).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jami al-Tawarikh artist, 1420-1430 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aq Qoyunlu government combined the customs of a steppe confederation with the bureaucratic traditions of Persian Islamic statecraft. Supreme power belonged to the Bayandur house, whose head took the titles of sultan and <em>padishah</em> and claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-khan">Oghuz Khan</a>, the legendary ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a>, an ideology celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-tihrani">Abu Bakr Tihrani</a>’s court chronicle, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitab-i-diyarbakriyya">Kitab-i Diyarbakriyya</a></em>. Real military power rested with the tribal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, who held lands and revenues in return for service and whose support was needed at every succession. Because there was no fixed rule of inheritance, the death of each ruler set brothers, sons and nephews against one another. Civil administration was run by a divan of mostly Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> and secretaries, who kept registers, collected taxes and drafted correspondence in Persian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> issued regulations on taxation and land, later remembered as the <em>kanun</em> of Hasan Padishah. Under Ya’qub (r. 1478–1490) the judge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi-isa-savaji">Qadi Isa Savaji</a> attempted a reform that aimed to revoke hereditary tax immunities, known as <em>soyurghal</em>, and to bring taxation closer to Islamic law. The reform alarmed the emirs and religious notables who benefited from the grants, and it was abandoned after Ya’qub’s death, showing the limits of central authority within the confederation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/venice"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/venice.jpg" alt="Venice" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venezia_aerial_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kallerna, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For a few decades the Aq Qoyunlu made western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> the seat of a major power that could challenge both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a>. Their contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> formed one of the earliest attempts at an alliance between a European state and an Iranian ruler against the Ottomans, and the reports of Venetian envoys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrogio-contarini">Ambrogio Contarini</a> are important sources for the region. The confederation’s most lasting legacy was the Safavid state. Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> through his daughter, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> took over the Aq Qoyunlu capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, much of their territory, their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribal army, which became the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, and many of their Persian bureaucrats and administrative practices. In eastern Anatolia the Ottomans kept the Aq Qoyunlu tax regulations in force after their conquest of the region, citing the law of Hasan Padishah in their provincial law codes for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a> and neighbouring districts. The court of Tabriz was also a centre of Persian culture. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a> dedicated his allegory <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaman-and-absal">Salaman and Absal</a></em> to Ya’qub, and the Turkmen style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> painting developed in Tabriz under Aq Qoyunlu patronage fed directly into the celebrated painting workshops of the early Safavid shahs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran.jpg" alt="Battle of Chaldiran" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_painting_in_Chehel_Sotoun1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amir Pashaei, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The weaknesses of the confederation surfaced after the death of Ya’qub in 1490. His young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baysunghur-aq-qoyunlu">Baysunghur</a> was soon displaced by his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a> (r. 1492–1497), who in turn was overthrown by Ahmad, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> who had grown up at the Ottoman court and who was killed within months after trying to curb the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>. Tribal factions backed rival princes, and by about 1500 the realm was divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvand-beg">Alvand</a>, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a>, and Murad, who ruled central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>. Years of civil war, plague and economic hardship eroded loyalty to the Bayandur house. Meanwhile the Safavid order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a>, whose leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar">Haydar</a> had been killed by Aq Qoyunlu forces in 1488, gathered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> followers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> around Haydar’s son Ismail. In 1501 Ismail defeated Alvand at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sharur">Battle of Sharur</a>, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and proclaimed himself shah. In 1503 he defeated Murad near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, and this victory is usually taken as the end of the Aq Qoyunlu state. Aq Qoyunlu partisans held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> until 1508 and Murad died in exile some years later, while Diyarbakır passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and then, after the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran">Battle of Chaldiran</a> in 1514, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Aq Qoyunlu turned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribal confederation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir">Diyarbakır</a> into the dominant power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>, challenged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and handed their capital, army and administration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> founded by Uzun Hasan’s grandson Ismail.</em></p>

